<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <%- include('const/head', { title: "Projects", description: "Selected projects: self-hosted web infrastructure, automation labs, Java game-server tooling, ANSYS SCADE embedded work, and robotics heritage.", canonical: "/projects" }) %>
</head>
<body>
  <%- include('const/header', { active: "projects" }) %>
  <main id="main" class="page">
    <h1>Projects</h1>
    <p class="sub">Things I designed and shipped — running, maintained, or honestly retired.</p>

    <section class="projects">
      <article class="project">
        <span class="tile t-teal" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100"><text x="50" y="66" class="tile-glyph">&lt;/&gt;</text></svg></span>
        <div>
          <h2>This site</h2>
          <p>Rebuilt November 2026. Node.js + Express + EJS on a home server;
             one stylesheet; zero client-side JavaScript; strict Content-Security-Policy
             (<code>script-src 'none'</code>) with HSTS and self-renewing Let's Encrypt
             certificates behind nginx. Every push is linted and unit-tested by CI
             before it can deploy; a canary re-checks the whole chain every ten minutes.
             The 2023 edition ignored <code>public/images/</code> and
             <code>public/videos/</code> in <code>.gitignore</code> — the media was never
             committed anywhere and did not survive. That mistake is fixed: media is
             versioned now (see <code>public/legacy-2023/</code> in git).</p>
          <p class="chips" aria-label="Stack">
            <span class="chip">Node.js</span><span class="chip">Express</span><span class="chip">EJS</span>
            <span class="chip">GitHub Actions</span><span class="chip">nginx</span><span class="chip">Let's Encrypt</span>
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="project">
        <span class="tile t-amber" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100"><text x="50" y="66" class="tile-glyph">OPS</text></svg></span>
        <div>
          <h2>Self-hosted automation &amp; local-AI lab</h2>
          <p>A small private fleet: Linux servers running CI runners for every repo,
             a monitoring canary that pages me by text message when anything goes red
             (and stays quiet when it's a false alarm), nightly backups verified by
             checksum with staleness alarms, split-DNS and certificate automation, and
             small local language models doing drafting, triage, and command execution
             — sized to run on consumer GPUs, with no cloud dependency.</p>
          <p class="chips" aria-label="Stack">
            <span class="chip">Linux</span><span class="chip">systemd</span><span class="chip">bash</span>
            <span class="chip">CI/CD</span><span class="chip">monitoring</span><span class="chip">llama.cpp / Ollama</span>
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="project">
        <span class="tile t-blue" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100"><text x="50" y="66" class="tile-glyph">MC</text></svg></span>
        <div>
          <h2>Game-server tooling (Java)</h2>
          <p>Custom Java plugins and an automated pipeline for a self-hosted
             Minecraft network: build → test matrix on throwaway servers → deploy →
             RCON health verification, with automatic rollback when a boot doesn't
             verify. Includes a live map compass plugin so players can find their way
             from the browser. Twelve-hour unattended failure hunts taught me more
             about preflight gates and honest status reporting than any manual.</p>
          <p class="chips" aria-label="Stack">
            <span class="chip">Java</span><span class="chip">Paper/Spigot API</span><span class="chip">CI/CD</span>
            <span class="chip">RCON</span><span class="chip">BlueMap</span>
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="project">
        <span class="tile t-violet" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100"><text x="50" y="66" class="tile-glyph">A</text></svg></span>
        <div>
          <h2>World Domination with ANSYS SCADE</h2>
          <p>A hands-on two-part series (published on the PADT engineering blog):
             model-based design from Simulink-style diagrams to embedded C running on
             an Arduino — what simulation-to-metal actually looks like when you drive
             the whole workflow yourself. <a href="/writing/wdws">Read the series index</a>.</p>
          <p class="chips" aria-label="Stack">
            <span class="chip">ANSYS SCADE</span><span class="chip">Model-based design</span>
            <span class="chip">Embedded C</span><span class="chip">Arduino</span>
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="project">
        <span class="tile t-green" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100"><text x="50" y="66" class="tile-glyph">3D</text></svg></span>
        <div>
          <h2>RoboArm &amp; Tello drone experiments <span class="era">2022–2023</span></h2>
          <p>A 3D-printed, CAD-designed robotic arm (Inventor → print → control
             code) and autonomous flight experiments on a DJI Tello. Their demo
             videos lived on the old site as MP4s; they didn't survive a drive change
             and are kept as a cautionary note — the arm and the drone still run.</p>
          <p class="chips" aria-label="Stack">
            <span class="chip">Autodesk Inventor</span><span class="chip">3D printing</span>
            <span class="chip">Python</span><span class="chip">drones</span>
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="project retired">
        <span class="tile t-slate" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100"><text x="50" y="66" class="tile-glyph">EV</text></svg></span>
        <div>
          <h2>Electric vehicle design &amp; capstone <span class="era">2017–2018</span></h2>
          <p>The heritage work: high-school EV design team, PLTW Engineering capstone,
             FIRST Robotics (team president, then mentor — 30 Under 30, CA 47th
             District). Most of what I trust about engineering starts here.</p>
          <p class="chips" aria-label="Stack">
            <span class="chip">SolidWorks</span><span class="chip">AutoCAD</span>
            <span class="chip">schematics</span><span class="chip">team lead</span>
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>
    </section>
  </main>
  <%- include('const/footer') %>
</body>
</html>
